WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:02.802
What do people mean by ‘agent’?

2
00:00:03.332 --> 00:00:06.381
‘Agent’ means different things
depending on who’s talking.

3
00:00:06.816 --> 00:00:11.207
Microsoft calls chatbots with custom
instructions ‘Copilot Agents’,

4
00:00:11.445 --> 00:00:13.457
even when they only answer questions.

5
00:00:14.112 --> 00:00:16.458
When people talk about coding agents,
they often mean

6
00:00:16.468 --> 00:00:19.006
a definition associated with Anthropic –

7
00:00:19.305 --> 00:00:22.186
models using tools in a loop
towards a goal.

8
00:00:22.528 --> 00:00:26.033
But normal Claude and Copilot chats
now work in a similar way,

9
00:00:26.314 --> 00:00:28.803
which is one reason why
token usage for those products

10
00:00:28.813 --> 00:00:30.949
is much higher than it used to be.

11
00:00:31.349 --> 00:00:34.223
So as a general definition,
this may be out of date.

12
00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:37.771
New products such as
Meta’s Muse and OpenAI’s Dots

13
00:00:37.781 --> 00:00:40.668
are popularising a new definition.

14
00:00:40.858 --> 00:00:43.998
This kind of agent is a chatbot
you give an ongoing job,

15
00:00:44.008 --> 00:00:47.237
and then it works in the background
and takes action on your behalf.

16
00:00:47.315 --> 00:00:48.950
For example, buying something.

17
00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:50.983
Why it matters.

18
00:00:51.572 --> 00:00:53.765
Understanding that there are
different definitions

19
00:00:53.775 --> 00:00:56.800
can help you critically assess
how the term is being used.

20
00:00:57.126 --> 00:00:59.059
Up this ladder, autonomy increases

21
00:00:59.069 --> 00:01:02.163
and so do the cognitive
and security risks.

22
00:01:02.323 --> 00:01:05.820
What role, if any, could each type
of agent have in your discipline?

23
00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.030
Try this.

24
00:01:07.483 --> 00:01:10.915
LSE doesn’t currently have a licence
for anything truly agentic,

25
00:01:10.925 --> 00:01:13.041
as most people now understand the term.

26
00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:16.198
The nearest thing is
a scheduled prompt in Copilot,

27
00:01:16.455 --> 00:01:18.108
though it can’t really act for you,

28
00:01:18.118 --> 00:01:21.261
and you should be aware that students
don’t have access to this feature.

29
00:01:22.136 --> 00:01:24.947
Send Copilot a prompt,
then hover over it.

30
00:01:25.017 --> 00:01:28.346
Select Schedule this prompt
and choose how often.

31
00:01:28.703 --> 00:01:33.091
For example, you could ask it to check
a web page you look after once a week

32
00:01:33.101 --> 00:01:34.388
for anything out of date,

33
00:01:36.783 --> 00:01:40.042
or to search for the last week’s news
on a topic you follow

34
00:01:40.052 --> 00:01:41.750
and list up to five items.
